WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00.680 --> 00:07.810
Last time we explored purpose, and what it means to define a purpose that includes both your needs and your audience’s.

2
00:08.580 --> 00:15.007
Today we’re going to be looking at insight, the third part of EPIC. Insight is important for a lot of reasons.

3
00:15.007 --> 00:18.630
Can you imagine what it’s like to communicate without insight?

4
00:19.240 --> 00:20.900
How terribly boring that must be.

5
00:21.610 --> 00:22.880
And what a waste of time.

6
00:23.490 --> 00:26.400
And I’m sure you’re busy, just like everyone else is.

7
00:27.080 --> 00:33.670
It is more critical than ever that we make the time count, make each interaction really matter.

8
00:34.310 --> 00:39.070
I think it’s important that we differentiate insight from data and information.

9
00:39.620 --> 00:43.970
There is a lot of noise out there, and more than ever we are bombarded with

10
00:44.740 --> 00:46.080
facts, figures,

11
00:46.440 --> 00:49.920
news, everything. How do we sort the wheat from the chaff?

12
00:51.110 --> 00:58.370
What really does make an insight? We can think about data as just the raw material that we have: numbers,

13
00:58.690 --> 01:00.160
anecdotes, what have you.

14
01:00.550 --> 01:06.439
Information is something where, let’s say, it’s been slightly more prepared or massaged in a way.

15
01:06.439 --> 01:12.510
But what does it take to really get to an insight? An insight actually requires time and reflection,

16
01:12.900 --> 01:15.940
and drawing out a sort of “so what?”

17
01:16.490 --> 01:22.560
Why is this relevant? What is compelling about it? Why does it make a difference to your audience?

18
01:23.170 --> 01:28.890
So let’s maintain a very high bar for what we mean by insight as we go through this section.

19
01:28.890 --> 01:34.050
The different ways you can think about insight as you work with the EPIC framework:

20
01:34.600 --> 01:41.220
the first is to ask yourself, what are the key insights that I want to bring to this meeting or interaction?

21
01:41.890 --> 01:45.190
You may have done a ton of work, a lot of analysis.

22
01:46.050 --> 01:48.160
Boiling the ocean is not helpful

23
01:48.770 --> 01:57.700
to bring to an interaction. You’ll want to think about how you structure and synthesize those ideas into a compelling insight.

24
01:58.340 --> 02:00.070
The second part is really around:

25
02:00.450 --> 02:05.950
what storyline is going to convey your insights in the most compelling and persuasive way?

26
02:07.560 --> 02:13.380
There are a lot of techniques we can use for storytelling, and don’t worry if you don’t feel like you’re naturally a good storyteller.

27
02:14.280 --> 02:15.650
It’s a learned skill.

28
02:16.170 --> 02:18.472
Storytellers aren’t born, they’re made.

29
02:18.472 --> 02:24.670
Lastly, the choice you make about what kind of visual aids and media you use is also a source of insight,

30
02:25.160 --> 02:28.870
because it can have a real impact on how your audience receives

31
02:29.220 --> 02:38.370
the information, but also how they act on it. Today, we will be looking at structuring, and how you can structure your message in a clear and compelling way.

32
02:38.760 --> 02:44.960
We will also explore the difference between summary and synthesis, which is critical to driving towards insight.

